      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The hamlet’s name honors Ellen Murray, eldest daughter of John and Harriet Murray, who sold 50‑acre homesteads to early settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ellenburg_depot_new_york",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1860s, Carter and Vestas moved a starch factory to Ellenburg Corners, boosting local agriculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofellenburgny.gov/history.html",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lumbering thrived south of the depot, employing up to 100 workers harvesting spruce and hemlock for pulp and shingles.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ellenburg_depot_new_york",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The first town hall in Ellenburg Center was built around 1853, marking the town’s administrative center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
